diff --git a/native-runtime/graphics/README.md b/native-runtime/graphics/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..99807340be4b752842d1ea76b607ccd58dd143b3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/native-runtime/graphics/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Native offscreen graphics fixture
+
+`native_graphics::RunOffscreenTriangle()` is independent of Core, HLE and guest memory. It loads the installed Windows Vulkan loader, selects a graphics queue and strictly reports unavailable capabilities or failed Vulkan calls. It uses existing Sirit to build minimal SPIR-V in memory; no shader compiler or dependency download occurs.
+
+The fixture creates two buffers (vertex upload and readback), an RGBA8 image, render pass, framebuffer and graphics pipeline. It records one three-vertex draw over a black clear, copies the image to readback, waits on a fence, and verifies all pixels, including red center/black corner. It intentionally requires host-visible coherent memory. It does not implement NVN, shader translation, presentation, guest dispatch or game graphics.
+
+Build this directory standalone with CMake/Ninja and the existing MSVC 14.50 toolchain. Existing dependency paths can be overridden with `NATIVE_GRAPHICS_VULKAN_INCLUDE` and `NATIVE_GRAPHICS_SIRIT_ROOT`. `native_graphics_probe` prints device/driver identity and verification counts; failures return nonzero. CTest timeout is 30 seconds; GPU fence timeout is 5 seconds.
+
+Source was authored locally. Sirit is an existing BSD-3-Clause dependency; Vulkan headers/loader keep their respective notices. No LASRecomp source or third-party shader bytecode was copied into this implementation.
+
+## Reusable resource API
+
+`vulkan_resources.h` exposes `VulkanDevice`, owning `PoolHandle` allocations and non-owning `BufferViewHandle` ranges. A pool owns a real Vulkan buffer, bound host-visible memory and its persistent mapping. Views retain the pool logically: pool destruction with live views fails; destroyed/cross-device/zero handles fail; pool/view sizes use subtraction bounds checks. Device destruction releases remaining views/pools. Methods require the creating thread; callers must destroy the device on that thread and must not race destruction. No guest pointer or NVN object layout is guessed.
+
+Upload copies into mapped Vulkan allocation and flushes noncoherent memory; Download invalidates before copying out. Explicit Flush/Invalidate support external synchronization boundaries, expanding ranges to `nonCoherentAtomSize` without crossing the allocation. Coherent memory needs no Vulkan flush/invalidate call. `prefer_noncoherent` selects a noncoherent compatible memory type when available; it is a preference, reported by PoolInfo, not a claim that every driver offers it. GPU execution completion must be established by a future submission/fence API before Download; this resource-only interface does not submit concurrent work.
+
+`require_device_address` requests Vulkan 1.2 and queries/enables bufferDeviceAddress, sets the buffer usage and allocation flags, then returns the actual driver-provided address. Without that explicit request, GetDeviceAddress fails. Unsupported flags, absent hardware/API features, invalid sizes/ranges, stale handles and live-view pool destruction throw errors; no fake GPU address or success stub is returned.
+
+`native_graphics_resource_test` checks actual allocation, aliased views, byte roundtrip, optional-memory preference, driver GPU address offset, cleanup, thread rejection, invalid ranges, overflow, unsupported flags and double destruction. The original offscreen draw remains a separate regression test; mapped-memory roundtrip alone is not GPU draw evidence.
+
+`GetBufferBinding` and `GetNativeHandles` return borrowed actual Vulkan handle bits for a future native command/NVN bridge, without requiring Vulkan headers in the public interface. They confer no ownership. Borrowers must not destroy these handles, must keep views/pools/device alive, and must synchronize every external GPU use before upload, download, pool destruction or device destruction. The backend cannot infer submissions made through borrowed handles. The device address is never substituted for a guest address.
+
+`VulkanHandles.enabled_shader_image_gather_extended` reports the feature actually enabled at device creation, after querying physical support. It is false when unavailable. The device enables only this supported feature in the core feature set; this does not enable other shader capabilities. Renderers must inspect each module's capabilities and reject a required feature when the corresponding enabled flag is false. Shader module creation by itself is insufficient evidence of pipeline feature compatibility.
diff --git a/native-runtime/graphics/native_graphics.cpp b/native-runtime/graphics/native_graphics.cpp
